  different field set — say [38;5;153m{email: {required, email}, bio: {maxLength: 200}, zip: {pattern}}[39m — against the same
  [38;5;153mvalidate()[39m. An optional [38;5;153mbio[39m and an optional-but-patterned [38;5;153mzip[39m exercise paths the login schema never touches. If the
  interface is wrong, that's where it shows.

  [1mBinder tests[0m are the honest gap, and there's a real fork here. Node has no DOM, so testing [38;5;153mbind()[39m means either
  bringing one in or faking one:

  - [1mjsdom as a devDependency[0m — real DOM semantics. Actually verifies the things most likely to be wrong: that the
    created [38;5;153m<span>[39m lands after the right input, that [38;5;153maria-describedby[39m points at a real id, that [38;5;153minput[39m events clear the
    right message. Costs the repo's first dependency and a lockfile.
  - [1mHand-rolled fake form object[0m — zero dependencies, consistent with the no-build choice. But the fake is code I write
    from my own assumptions about the DOM, so it tests the binder against my beliefs rather than against a browser.
    Insertion and aria behavior are exactly what a fake gets wrong.
  - [1mCore only; verify the binder by hand in a browser[0m — least work, and defensible for ~60 lines of wiring. But nothing
    catches a binder regression later.

  I'd recommend jsdom. The binder is the part of this design with genuine DOM behavior, and a fake DOM gives you the
  [3mfeeling[0m of coverage over the code most likely to break. One devDependency is a fair price; it never ships to the page,
  which still loads two plain [38;5;153m<script>[39m tags.

